<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Datasets.</span> Our experiments are conducted on two datasets to evaluate the effectiveness of the algorithm in both in-domain and out-of-domain settings: (1) Geometry3K focused on geometric problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2021a)</a></cite>, (2) a 6k out-of-domain dataset randomly sampled from the dataset proposed in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, which randomly sampled from established open-source resources, including Geometry3K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2021a)</a></cite>, GeoQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite>, GeoQA-Plus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao &amp; Xiao (2022)</a></cite>, Geos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seo et al. (2015)</a></cite>, AI2D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kembhavi et al. (2016)</a></cite>, TQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2018)</a></cite>, FigureQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahou et al. (2017)</a></cite>, TabMWP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2022b)</a></cite>, ChartQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masry et al. (2022)</a></cite>, IconQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2021b)</a></cite>, Clevr-Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lindström &amp; Abraham (2022)</a></cite>, M3CoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and ScienceQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2022a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Benchmarks.</span> Our experiments consist of two parts. First, we evaluate the overall performance of the algorithms on four open multimodal math benchmarks: MathVision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, MathVista <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2023)</a></cite>, MathVerse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and We-Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al. (2024)</a></cite>. These benchmarks cover diverse problem types such as geometry, charts, and tables, spanning multiple subjects and levels of visual reasoning difficulty. Second, we conduct a detailed analysis for algorithms on the Geometry3K dataset to examine the algorithmic behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare our models with a range of advanced multimodal large language models. These include GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al. (2024)</a></cite> and Claude-3.5-Sonnet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2024)</a></cite>, which are state-of-the-art closed-source MLLMs. QvQ-Preview-72B[2]
and Skywork R1V-32B  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2025)</a></cite> are multimodal large reasoning models specifically designed for R1-like chain-of-thought capabilities. At the 7B scale, we compare with MM-Eureka-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al. (2025)</a></cite>, OpenVLThinker-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2025)</a></cite>, ThinkLite-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, and VLAA-Thinker-Qwen2.5VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. All of these 7B models are based on Qwen2.5-VL and are enhanced for mathematical reasoning via fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, or iterative self-improvement techniques. In addition, we compare the performance of GRPO and DAPO on the Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct model to assess the impact of different strategies under consistent model and training settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation details.</span>
Our work is based on EasyR1 and VeRL framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yaowei et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al. (2025)</a></cite>. We initialize all policy models with Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct and conduct experiments on 8 A800 GPUs (40GB). Unless otherwise specified, we follow the default EasyR1 settings: maximum response length of 2048, global batch size 128, rollout batch size 512, rollout <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>=5, temperature 1.0, and learning rate <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. GRPO and our method adopt <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{low}=\epsilon_{high}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{low}=\epsilon_{high}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and a binary reward to avoid relative advantage bias (see detail in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Relative Advantage Bias of GRPO ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), while DAPO includes <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{high}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{high}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> and an additional length-aware linear penalty (up to 0.5) for responses exceeding 1536 tokens. For our method, we perform additional rollouts with <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}=20</annotation></semantics></math>, temperature = 1.2; responses with absolute advantage larger than 1 are stored in a replay buffer of size 5120, with experience replay triggered every 5 steps.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>The performance comparison on the Geometry3K dataset shows that our method achieves a significant improvement over existing approaches.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Models</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Qwen2.5-VL-Instruct</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Ours</td>
