</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main results</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/GEO3k-acc.png" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/132;" width="176" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/GEO3k-ent.png" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/132;" width="176" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/GEO3k-len.png" id="S5.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/132;" width="176" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>On the Geometry3K dataset, our method not only achieves excellent performance but also demonstrates superior exploration capability and training stability.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance.</span> In our experiments, we employ the Exploration-Filtering-Replay framework, yielding notable improvements on both out-of-domain and in-domain datasets. As shown in Table 1, the proposed method delivers consistently strong results across four multimodal reasoning benchmarks, with an average gain of +5.66 over the base model (Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct), outperforming GRPO by 19.9%. On the in-domain Geometry3K dataset, the improvement is even more pronounced: Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Experimental setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports a +16.80 gain, representing a 36.5% relative advantage over GRPO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2 Main results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our framework maintains the policy entropy around 1, striking a balance between sufficient exploration and exploitation. In the later training stages, when the vanilla GRPO tends to suffer from vanishing advantages, our method further increases entropy to encourage continued exploration of informative reward signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stability.</span> Our framework leverages additional rollouts to enrich reward signals, while an online filtering mechanism mitigates training variance by excluding low-quality samples. Extensive experiments reveal that the proposed method circumvents collapse issues—occasionally encountered in GRPO and DAPO. Furthermore, the steadily increasing response length during training reflects the model’s progressively improved reasoning ability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Ablation Studies</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the contribution of each component in our framework, we conduct an ablation study to examine whether all three modules—additional rollout, online filtering, and replay buffer—are all essential for achieving the observed improvements in stability and overall performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Additional Rollout</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Disabling the additional rollout module exposes the model to a fundamental trade-off inherent in GRPO-based training. When the number of samples <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is small, the reward signal is constrained by the base model’s pass@<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, limiting the model’s ability to solve challenging problems and suppressing the emergence of “aha moments.”</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 1</span></span><span id="Thmclaim1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmclaim1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-quality samples have the potential to activate deeper reasoning abilities within the model.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Additional Rollout ‣ 6 Ablation Studies ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the model’s reward on the training set plateaus at a relatively low level, restricting further gains in test accuracy. In the early stages of training, the entropy remains low, indicating limited exploration. However, once the model hits this performance bottleneck, entropy begins to rise—yet this delayed exploration yields minimal returns, suggesting that the model struggles to escape local optima. The additional rollout module is specifically designed to break this situation, enabling targeted deep exploration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/ab-acc.png" id="S6.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:165/124;" width="165" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/ab-pas.png" id="S6.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:165/124;" width="165" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/ab-ent.png" id="S6.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:165/124;" width="165" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The ablation study conducted on the Geometry3K dataset not only validates the effectiveness of our proposed method but also reveals the distinct roles played by samples of varying quality during the training process.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Online Filtering</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Removing the online filtering module results in significant instability and inefficiency during training. Without filtering, the model treats all samples—regardless of their advantage—as equally important, allowing zero-advantage samples to increase gradient variance and low-quality negative trajectories to distorb the learning signal. This problem is further exacerbated during the additional rollout phase, where abundant incorrect samples significantly amplifies gradient variance, making it difficult for the model to retain meaningful patterns from rare high-quality responses. As a result, training becomes slower, more chaotic, and more prone to divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 2</span></span><span id="Thmclaim2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmclaim2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-quality samples from challenging prompts often mislead optimization, causing the model to converge prematurely to suboptimal solutions and impeding further learning.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Additional Rollout ‣ 6 Ablation Studies ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, during the early stages of training, the inclusion of a large number of negative samples significantly boosts the model’s performance on the training set, but yields little improvement on the test set. This indicates that most low-quality samples lead the model to converge more quickly to a local optimum, offering little benefit to its generalization ability.
Moreover, the forward and backward computation of these negative samples incurs substantial overhead, reducing the sample efficiency of the overall training process.
Online filtering plays a crucial role in maintaining gradient clarity and training stability by selectively preserving informative samples and discarding neutral or low-impact ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Replay Buffer</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the replay buffer is disabled, we observe a drastic drop in performance, even falling below that of the vanilla GRPO baseline, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Additional Rollout ‣ 6 Ablation Studies ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This counterintuitive result reveals that the abundant high-quality samples generated during the additional rollout phase can actually become detrimental without a proper replay mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 3</span></span><span id="Thmclaim3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmclaim3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">While high-quality samples may induce high-entropy exploration, incorporating experience replay enables the model to retain and consolidate useful gradients, thus restoring training stability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As high-quality samples typically have low generation probabilities, rewarding them shifts the model’s response distribution toward low-probability but correct outputs, disrupting the convergence trajectory and consequently increasing entropy. Due to their high advantage values and low sampling probabilities, high-quality samples are more likely to have their gradients clipped during training. In the absence of a replay buffer, such valuable samples contribute only short-lived and unstable gradients that are quickly overwritten. Instead of reinforcing beneficial behavior, this transient gradient signal increases policy uncertainty, pushing the model into a state of chaotic, high-entropy exploration, ultimately harming both learning stability and performance. To address this issue, we leverage experience replay to ensure that high-quality samples continuously provide effective gradients, enabling the model to converge along the new trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion and Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we investigate the impact of sample quality on RL for large language models, with a particular focus on addressing challenging prompts. Our findings show that low-quality incorrect samples—those with low absolute advantage—tend to trap the model in local optima and offer little or even negative contribution to training. In contrast, high-quality samples with large advantages play a critical role in guiding the model through a meaningful transition from entropy-driven exploration to entropy-reducing convergence. This process is essential for eliciting deeper reasoning capabilities within the model.
To this end, we propose an <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration-Filtering-Replay</span> framework that emphasizes the discovery, selection, and sustained utilization of high-quality samples. Our method introduces additional rollouts to enhance reward density, employs online filtering to suppress noisy samples, and integrates experience replay to amplify the impact of high-quality samples across training iterations. This strategy not only helps transform isolated “aha moments” into a sustained “aha state,” but also shifts reinforcement learning from a “reinforcement lottery” toward a more structured and reliable process of reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
