<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appendix
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:14.2pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A0.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22200v1/img/bias.png" id="A0.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/190;" width="494" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>During training with GRPO, when the primary reward signal (accuracy) vanishes, other auxiliary rewards become dominant, which introduces bias.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Relative Advantage Bias of GRPO </h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In GRPO, computing relative advantages within a group can introduce bias, especially when the reward becomes multi-dimensional. For example, as shown in Fig. <a href="#A0.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filtering-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, if the reward comprises both accuracy and auxiliary signals such as length, format, or entropy, a significant issue arises when the accuracy signal vanishes within a group—i.e., when all responses are either correct or incorrect. In such cases, auxiliary rewards (e.g., length) dominate the advantage computation. This may result in incorrect responses being rewarded simply for being shorter, or correct responses being penalized for being longer. Such misleading gradients introduce irreversible noise into training and lead to reward hacking. Therefore, in the training involved in this paper, we use binary rewards whenever possible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Key Hyperparameters</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The quantity of high-quality samples varies significantly with dataset difficulty, which makes our algorithm sensitive to three key hyperparameters: <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">clip higher, replay buffer size, and replay frequency</span>. This is because the gradients from high-quality samples must be just perfect—between underfitting, proper fitting, and overfitting—to be effective. Notably, on relatively simple datasets such as Math, where high-quality samples are inherently scarce, the improvements of our framework over the original GRPO are less pronounced.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
